# apt-get — classic APT front end (script-safe)

`apt-get` keeps stable output across versions, so prefer it over `apt` inside scripts.

## Fetch package list

```sh
apt-get update
```

## Install updates

```sh
# Updates only
apt-get upgrade

# Updates AND install/remove packages as needed
apt-get dist-upgrade

# Full command
apt-get update && apt-get dist-upgrade
```

## Install a new package

```sh
apt-get install package
```

## Download a package without installing it

```sh
# The .deb lands in your current working dir
apt-get download modsecurity-crs
```

## Change cache dir and archive dir (where .deb files are stored)

```sh
apt-get -o Dir::Cache="/path/to/destination/dir/" -o Dir::Cache::archives="./" install package
```

## Show apt-get installed packages

```sh
grep 'install ' /var/log/dpkg.log
```

## Real-world: unattended patching in a cron/systemd timer script

```sh
export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update -qq && apt-get dist-upgrade -y -qq && apt-get autoremove -y -qq
```

| Flag | What it does                       |
| ---- | ---------------------------------- |
| -y   | assume yes                         |
| -qq  | very quiet (script logs stay lean) |
| -s   | simulate, change nothing           |
| -f   | fix broken dependencies            |
